{"author":"Rebar Bot Nava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\n\nPublication of the reclaimed `tickets` history (epic `polite-antivirus-bedbug`) is held until enforced `verify-identity --all` (`REBAR_IDENTITY_REQUIRE_AUTHENTICATED=1`, no `enforce_since`) passes outright on the rewritten history. On origin/tickets 37033c41613 it fails: 874 events are unsigned (621 written by the operator Mac before it had a signing key, 253 written with no `author_id`), and about 7,000 SNAPSHOT ledger entries fail because the MCP server signed them with op-cert keys that are not on identity `594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d`'s keyring. Operator decision (2026-11-19, recorded on the epic): re-sign every failing event during the rewrite with the bot key that was on 594c's keyring when the event was written, and give the 253 events `author_id` 594c, because every writer in that group acts as that identity.\n\n## Approach\n\nAdd an optional re-sign step to the existing offline collapse engine (`collapse_shadow_tracker`). All signatures are produced and checked before the rewrite stream starts, and the stream only substitutes bytes, so a signing failure can never leave a half-written rewrite.\n\n1. New module `src/rebar/_commands/reclaim_resign.py`.\n   - `build_resign_plan(shadow_tracker, failing, key_files, *, identity_id, boundary)` returns a `ResignPlan`. Inputs: the shadow tracker at its current tip; `failing`, the parsed array printed by enforced `verify-identity --all --format json` on that same tip (each entry has `event_uuid`, `ticket_id`); `key_files`, a mapping from keyring public-key text to a public-key file whose private half is held by the ssh-agent at `SSH_AUTH_SOCK`; the identity id; and the collapse boundary.\n   - Target matching. A failing event whose uuid names an active raw event file (`<ticket>/<timestamp>-<uuid>-<TYPE>.json`) at the tip becomes a raw target, keyed by that file's blob id (event files are append-only, so the blob id names the event everywhere in history). Otherwise it is looked up by `event_uuid` in the `authorship_ledger` of the active SNAPSHOT files at the tip and becomes a ledger target. A failing event matched by neither raises `ResignError` listing every unmatched uuid. The plan records the tip it was built at.\n   - Event position. A raw event's position is `{timestamp}-{uuid}`, the same string `verify_authorship._event_from_file` uses; a ledger target's position is its entry's `position.position`.\n   - Key choice reuses the single era rule `attest.authorship.keys_valid_at_anchor`, evaluated on the ORIGINAL graph of the shadow tracker (resolvers from `authorship.build_position_commit_map` and `authorship.build_ancestry_oracle`, anchored at the event's introducing commit). If the event's introducing commit is an ancestor of the boundary, a key whose KEY_ADD is also below the boundary must additionally satisfy `added_at <= event position`, because after the collapse both resolve to the checkpoint commit and the verifier orders them by position. Among the keys that pass and were supplied, the one with the latest `added_at` is used (the key that was current when the event was written). A target with no such key raises `ResignError` with the count of affected events, before any signing.\n   - Pre-flight. Every supplied public key must appear in `ssh-add -L` output for the current agent; otherwise `ResignError`. Then every target is signed up front: raw targets get `author_id` set to the identity when missing, a new `author_sig` from `authorship.sign_event_authorship(event, public_key_file, principal=identity_id)` (`ssh-keygen -Y sign -f <public key file>` signs through the agent), and are serialized with `rebar._store.canonical.canonical_bytes`; ledger targets get `signature` = DSSE envelope over `authorship.build_authorship_statement(event_uuid, content_hash)` and `signer_pubkey` = the chosen key. Each new raw signature is checked with `authorship.verify_authorship_any_key` before the plan is returned. Any `ssh-keygen` failure raises `ResignError` naming the target. No ref or object is written by plan construction.\n2. `reclaim_collapse.py`: `collapse_shadow_tracker(..., resign: ResignPlan | None = None)`. `None` keeps today's output byte for byte.\n   - With a plan, the collapse first asserts the branch tip equals the plan's tip, else `ResignError`.\n   - `_StreamedRewrite._write_base`: both the checkpoint commit and the ledger-anchor commit (which today re-emit the original boundary tree with `M 040000 <tree>`) append an inline override for every boundary-tree path whose blob id is a raw target, after the tree line, so the anchor (first parent of every replayed commit) carries the signed bytes.\n   - `_diff_lines`: an `M` line whose blob id is a raw target is emitted as an inline override instead, covering events introduced above the boundary, renames to `.retired`, and merges.\n   - `_rewrite_ledger_in_event` re-signs ledger targets after the existing `commit_sha` re-resolution and counts a re-sign as a change, so a SNAPSHOT whose only change is a re-sign is still written through the existing inline path. `CollapseResult` gains `events_resigned` and `ledger_entries_resigned`.\n   - Before the shadow branch is updated, the collapse checks that every raw target blob present at the original tip was substituted and every ledger target was re-signed at least once; a shortfall raises `ResignError`, deletes the scratch ref and leaves the branch unchanged. All `ResignError`s are fatal for that run and safe to retry after fixing the cause, since the branch is never moved.\n3. No CLI flag, config key or environment variable is added. The re-sign is a library parameter driven from the operator's publication runbook, like `reclaim_publish.publish_git_ref`. `reclaim_dry_run.py` is not changed: its `reducer_equal` and equal-counts checks assume a verdict-preserving collapse, so the real-data proof uses a scratch driver outside the repository that calls the same functions and records per-step durations.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `src/rebar/_commands/reclaim_resign.py` (new)\n- `src/rebar/_commands/reclaim_collapse.py`\n- `tests/unit/test_reclaim_resign.py` (new)\n\n## Testing\n\nTest-first, on synthetic stores under `tmp_path` in the style of `tests/unit/test_reclaim_collapse.py`, with ed25519 keys generated by `ssh-keygen` in `tmp_path` and loaded into an ssh-agent the test fixture starts and kills (skipped when `ssh-agent` or ssh-keygen >= 8.9 is absent).\n- Happy path. An identity whose CREATE has no keys, then KEY_ADD of key A at timestamp 1100 and KEY_ADD of key B at timestamp 3000 (above the boundary). Targets: an unsigned raw event at 1500 below the boundary, an unsigned raw event at 3500 above it, an unsigned raw event at 3600 with no `author_id`, and a SNAPSHOT ledger entry at 1200 with `signer_pubkey: null`. After `collapse_shadow_tracker(..., resign=plan, apply=True)`, enforced `verify-identity --all` with no `enforce_since` exits 0 with 0 unsigned; the 1500 and 1200 events are signed by key A and the 3500 and 3600 events by key B (asserted from the SSHSIG public key in each envelope); the 3600 event carries `author_id`.\n- Reduced state equals the pre-collapse state apart from the `authorship` counts and the added `author_id`.\n- Failure paths, each leaving the shadow branch at its original tip: a target at timestamp 900, before key A, raises `ResignError` with count 1; a failing uuid that is neither a raw file nor a ledger entry raises `ResignError` naming it; a supplied key that is not loaded in the agent raises `ResignError`; a plan built at a different tip is refused by the collapse.\n- Edge: `resign=None` produces the same rewritten tip tree as the current engine; an empty failing list yields a plan that changes nothing; the same SNAPSHOT carried across later commits is signed once (signing calls counted through a wrapped signer).\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] `tests/unit/test_reclaim_resign.py` passes the happy, failure and edge cases above, and `tests/unit/test_reclaim_collapse.py` passes unchanged.\n- [ ] `make verify` is green; `python scripts/check_mechanism_delta.py --check` and `python scripts/check_complexity_baseline.py --check` exit 0; no file under `src/rebar` exceeds 800 lines.\n- [ ] The Gerrit change is merged with `LLM-Review +1` and `Verified +1`.\n- [ ] [non-codebase] On a fresh no-remote fetch of current origin/tickets, enforced `verify-identity --all` (no `enforce_since`) on the rewritten history reports 0 non-verified events and exits 0, reduced state equals the original apart from `authorship` counts and the added `author_id`s, and the counts plus per-step durations are posted on `polite-antivirus-bedbug`. No production swap is executed. provenance: environment=developer-workstation-scratch-dir; principal=RebarBotNava; privilege_posture=narrower; instrument=simulation"}},"env_id":"492a5c71-6cda-48a6-8134-02ff6a672739","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1795127247534164001,"uuid":"47f9f519-aeea-4273-9992-945131bb5064"}